"Resizes geographic regions to encode a quantity while deliberately trading away some shape, distance or positional fidelity.\n\nUse when: Make the geographic distribution of totals visible without large-area regions dominating. Compare regions by population or another explicit extensive quantity.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need accurate distance, shape, navigation or exact boundary interpretation.\n\nVisual grammar: Region area represents the chosen quantity under a declared cartogram method. Labels or reference geography maintain recognizable identity. Color may encode a separate variable only with an independent legend.\n\nBuild: Choose the quantity and explain why geographic distortion helps. Select a method with explicit shape and adjacency tradeoffs. Provide clear region identities and a small geographic reference if needed.\n\nCheck: Displayed area corresponds to the chosen quantity within method limits. The map clearly discloses that geography is distorted. Lost adjacencies or altered positions are not treated as factual connections.\n\nPitfalls: Calling a cartogram a scale-accurate reference map. Using radius instead of area to size circular region representations.\n\nExample: A population cartogram expands densely populated regions and reduces sparsely populated ones, retaining labels and a geographic reference inset.\n\nAlternatives: Proportional-symbol map, Choropleth map."
